  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 325 AM CDT Mon Sep 21 2026\n\nSome hi-res guidance is suggesting that there may be some\nadditional pop-up showers/storm chances continuing into Tuesday,\nbut capped rain chances at locally 10-20% due to overall\nsubsidence expected to be in place behind the upper-level\ndisturbance. \n\nLong-range guidance is in fairly good agreement that the upper-\nlevel low will stall and strengthen over the northeast US by \nthe end of the week. The ridging over the Southern Plains that \nwas flattened by this weekend's shortwave intrusion will build \nback to the north through the week, with the potential for\nseveral embedded shortwaves to ride atop the ridge between now\nand next weekend. The upper air pattern is a little messy \nbetween now and Wednesday, which causes some uncertainty later\nin the period with respect to what happens as various open waves\nare absorbed into the polar jet, but it appears that the next\nbig trough will push into the West Coast later this week and\nbegin de-amplifying the ridge, with a return of 15-30% PoPs to\nour far western counties Thursday night into Friday. When the\nridge does start getting broken down later this week, our next\nrainfall chances will return, but much still TBD with respect to\nwhen and whether widespread measurable rainfall can be expected.\n\nNortherly flow will prevent significant changes in temperatures\nmost of the week, with highs in the mid-70s to low 80s through\nFriday and overnight lows in the 50s. Temperature spreads begin\nto increase on Saturday and beyond as model solutions diverge on\nthe evolution of the dominant ridge aloft.",
